# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
/target/

# Generated by Tauri
# will have schema files for capabilities auto-completion
/gen/schemas

# The Android Gradle project IS committed — it is a real project with real hand-edits (the
# manifest's permission list, the icons, later the signing config), and regenerating it with
# `android init` would silently drop them. What is not committed is what a build produces, and
# the template's own `gen/android/.gitignore` and `gen/android/app/.gitignore` already cover
# almost all of it: `.gradle`, `build`, `local.properties` (which names THIS machine's SDK
# path), `key.properties`, `keystore.properties`, `/.tauri`, `/tauri.settings.gradle`, and on
# the app side the built `.so` files, the copied `tauri.conf.json`, `tauri.build.gradle.kts`,
# `proguard-tauri.pro` and `tauri.properties`.
#
# **`app/tauri.properties` is deliberately among them** because `autoIncrementVersionCode` is
# off; the `AndroidConfig` docs say to un-ignore it only when that flag is turned on.
#
# What the template misses is the keystore FILE. A `.properties` naming a password is ignored,
# the `.jks` holding the key is not — and that key is the one secret in this project that
# cannot be regenerated: lose it and the app can never be updated under the same Play listing
# again, publish it and anyone can. The lines are here rather than in the task that creates a
# key, so the trap is closed before the key exists.
/gen/android/**/*.jks
/gen/android/**/*.keystore
